    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(5))  # F0..F4\n    customers = list(range(15))  # C0..C14\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(35, 67), (48, 1), (89, 31), (8, 97), (84, 21)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(41, 73), (18, 2), (68, 38), (5, 62), (23, 70), (33, 77), (3, 55), (46, 38), (57, 66), (32, 78), (85, 37), (96, 17), (98, 14), (70, 50), (16, 71)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [140.0, 145.0, 139.0, 187.0, 174.0]\n    customer_demands = [45.0, 39.0, 33.0, 49.0, 46.0, 48.0, 25.0, 28.0, 38.0, 30.0, 35.0, 11.0, 47.0, 24.0, 18.0]\n    fixed_costs = [176.0, 204.0, 357.0, 153.0, 432.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Deciding which warehouses to open and how to serve customers at the lowest total cost\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point on our distribution footprint. We have five candidate warehouse sites and fifteen customer destinations, and we need to determine which sites to activate and how to route shipments so that every customer is served reliably and we control spend.\n\nThe candidate warehouse locations are provided as map coordinates in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (35, 67)\n  - F1: (48, 1)\n  - F2: (89, 31)\n  - F3: (8, 97)\n  - F4: (84, 21), and the customer delivery points are listed in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (41, 73)\n  - C1: (18, 2)\n  - C2: (68, 38)\n  - C3: (5, 62)\n  - C4: (23, 70)\n  - C5: (33, 77)\n  - C6: (3, 55)\n  - C7: (46, 38)\n  - C8: (57, 66)\n  - C9: (32, 78)\n  - C10: (85, 37)\n  - C11: (96, 17)\n  - C12: (98, 14)\n  - C13: (70, 50)\n  - C14: (16, 71). Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates. Opening any warehouse carries a fixed cost as outlined in [176.0, 204.0, 357.0, 153.0, 432.0]. Once a site is open, moving product to customers incurs transportation charges based on how far the shipment travels and how many units we ship, using the per\u2011unit, per\u2011kilometer rate in 0.5. Each warehouse has a throughput limit shown in [140.0, 145.0, 139.0, 187.0, 174.0], and each customer has a required order volume that must be fulfilled in full, as given in [45.0, 39.0, 33.0, 49.0, 46.0, 48.0, 25.0, 28.0, 38.0, 30.0, 35.0, 11.0, 47.0, 24.0, 18.0].\n\nKey business rules:\n- A facility can only ship if it\u2019s opened.\n- Each customer\u2019s demand must be completely satisfied; no shorting or backorders.\n- No facility may ship more than its capacity.\n- Shipping costs scale with both distance (measured as Euclidean, straight-line) and volume, at the stated transport rate.\n\nOur decisions are twofold:\n- Select which of the five candidate warehouses to open.\n- Determine how much to ship from each open warehouse to each of the fifteen customers.\n\nOur objective is straightforward: minimize total cost by balancing the fixed cost of opening warehouses with the variable cost of shipping, given the distance metric and the capacity and demand realities. Once we align on these inputs and assumptions, we can generate a recommended network plan that meets all customer needs at the lowest achievable cost.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Deciding which warehouses to open and how to serve customers at the lowest total cost\n\nTeam,\n\nWe\u2019re at a decision point on our distribution footprint. We have five candidate warehouse sites and fifteen customer destinations, and we need to determine which sites to activate and how to route shipments so that every customer is served reliably and we control spend.\n\nThe candidate warehouse locations are provided as map coordinates in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and the customer delivery points are listed in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Distances between facilities and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates. Opening any warehouse carries a fixed cost as outlined in {FIXED_COSTS}. Once a site is open, moving product to customers incurs transportation charges based on how far the shipment travels and how many units we ship, using the per\u2011unit, per\u2011kilometer rate in {TRANSPORT_RATE}. Each warehouse has a throughput limit shown in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}, and each customer has a required order volume that must be fulfilled in full, as given in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}.\n\nKey business rules:\n- A facility can only ship if it\u2019s opened.\n- Each customer\u2019s demand must be completely satisfied; no shorting or backorders.\n- No facility may ship more than its capacity.\n- Shipping costs scale with both distance (measured as Euclidean, straight-line) and volume, at the stated transport rate.\n\nOur decisions are twofold:\n- Select which of the five candidate warehouses to open.\n- Determine how much to ship from each open warehouse to each of the fifteen customers.\n\nOur objective is straightforward: minimize total cost by balancing the fixed cost of opening warehouses with the variable cost of shipping, given the distance metric and the capacity and demand realities. Once we align on these inputs and assumptions, we can generate a recommended network plan that meets all customer needs at the lowest achievable cost."
